WEBVTT

00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:40.790
<v Ira Glass>Your know who Studs Terkel is? I think a lot of people, especially if you're younger, you read him in school-- his books, Division Street and Working and The Good War . He was an oral historian. But from 1952 to 1997, he also did a radio show on WMFT in Chicago, where he would broadcast the interviews that ended up in his books, and also lots of interviews with famous musicians and actors and artists. Anyway, I was going through some old tapes of his radio show recently for this event they had celebrating his life. And I stumbled across this utterly ordinary, nothing-special-going-on Friday on his radio program. Anyway, here's Studs.
<v Ira Glass>你知道Studs Terkel是谁吗？我想很多人，特别是年轻人，在学校里读过他的书——比如《分区街》（Division Street）、《工作》（Working）和《好战争》（The Good War）。他是一位口述历史学家。但在1952年到1997年间，他还在芝加哥的WMFT电台主持一档广播节目，在那里他会播出那些最终被收录进他书中的采访，以及许多对著名音乐家、演员和艺术家的采访。总之，最近为了一个庆祝他生平的活动，我在翻看他广播节目的一些旧录音带。我偶然发现了他在节目中一个极其普通、平平无奇的周五。总之，这就是Studs。

00:00:40.790 --> 00:00:48.240
<v Studs Terkel>I think it's pretty obvious to readers that Mike Royko's column is not only witty, but I think it's journalism in the true sense, in digging into--
<v Studs Terkel>我想读者们都很清楚，Mike Royko的专栏不仅风趣幽默，而且我认为那是真正意义上的新闻报道，深入挖掘——

00:00:48.240 --> 00:01:06.750
<v Ira Glass>It's a Friday in July nearly 50 years ago. It's July 29, 1966. And his guest that day is a good friend of his, newspaper columnist Mike Royko, another iconic Chicago figure. Anyway, on this particular day, they kill two hours very interestingly, I have to say, chatting about stories in the news.
<v Ira Glass>那是近50年前七月的一个周五，1966年7月29日。那天他的嘉宾是他的好朋友、报纸专栏作家Mike Royko，另一位标志性的芝加哥人物。总之，在那特别的一天，不得不说，他们非常有趣地消磨了两个小时，聊着新闻里的故事。

00:01:06.750 --> 00:01:33.580
<v Ira Glass>The medical director of Cook County hospital, for example, had made headlines when he said that he did not need to air condition the operating rooms in the facility. Surgeons, he said, should instead eat salted crackers, which would replace the salt that doctors lose when they sweat. There was some news that week about the prize fighter they still called Cassius Clay. There was a story that was national news at the time that I would be very surprised if you heard of. I had not heard of this.
<v Ira Glass>例如，库克县医院的医疗主任在说他不需要给医院的手术室安装空调时上了头条。他说外科医生应该多吃咸饼干，这样可以补充医生出汗流失的盐分。那一周还有一些关于那位当时仍被称为Cassius Clay的拳击手的新闻。当时还有一个全国性的新闻故事，如果你们听说过我会非常惊讶。我自己之前没听说过。

00:01:33.580 --> 00:01:47.790
<v Ira Glass>Basically, a justice on the Supreme Court, William O. Douglas, a liberal appointed by FDR who'd been on the bench almost three decades at that point-- the news was he had gotten married. Here's Studs talking to Mike Royko about that.
<v Ira Glass>基本上，最高法院大法官William O. Douglas，一位由富兰克林·罗斯福总统任命的自由派，当时已经在任近三十年了——新闻是他结婚了。下面是Studs和Mike Royko谈论这件事。

00:01:47.790 --> 00:01:56.070
<v Studs Terkel>And you did a column, I understand, received quite a response. What happened in Congress, the objections of many-- Pat, I think this is worth reading.
<v Studs Terkel>我听说你写了一篇专栏，反响很大。国会里发生了什么，许多人的反对——Pat，我觉得这值得一读。

00:01:56.070 --> 00:01:56.670
<v Male Speaker>Mhm.
<v Male Speaker>嗯。

00:01:56.670 --> 00:02:00.990
<v Studs Terkel>May I read this one? Because this leads, I know, to all sorts of discussion.
<v Studs Terkel>我可以读这一篇吗？因为我知道，这会引出各种各样的讨论。

00:02:00.990 --> 00:02:20.670
<v Ira Glass>OK, I'm just going to stop the tape right there. For whatever reason, Royko seems to prefer that Studs read his columns on this show, rather than read them out loud himself. And if it's OK with you, I enjoyed this next bit, and I know that you tuned in just now to hear a radio show made, you know, today, but let's just do a little time travel, OK?
<v Ira Glass>好的，我就在这儿暂停录音。不管出于什么原因，Royko似乎更喜欢让Studs在节目中读他的专栏，而不是他自己大声读出来。如果你们不介意的话，我挺喜欢接下来的这一段，我知道你们刚才调到这里是想听一个“今天”制作的广播节目，但让我们稍微做个时间旅行，好吗？

00:02:20.670 --> 00:02:31.630
<v Ira Glass>So your radio or your computer or your smartphone or whatever picks up instead a radio show from 1966 for just a couple minutes. And let's enjoy this together. Here's Studs.
<v Ira Glass>所以你们的收音机、电脑、智能手机或其他什么设备，会在这几分钟里播放1966年的广播节目。让我们一起来享受吧。这就是Studs。

00:02:31.630 --> 00:02:54.990
<v Studs Terkel>And Mike writes, "a ringing dissent to criticism of Justice Douglas. When Associate Justice William O. Douglas returns from his honeymoon, it would be perfectly proper for him to do the following-- walk with great dignity to his seat on the Supreme Court bench, sit down, draw his black robes about him, slowly raise  is right hand, touch the tip of his thumb to the tip of his nose, wiggle his fingers."
<v Studs Terkel>Mike写道：“对批评Douglas大法官的言论表示强烈反对。当威廉·O·道格拉斯大法官度完蜜月回来时，他做以下行为是完全得体的——庄重地走到他在最高法院的席位上，坐下，披上他的黑袍，慢慢举起右手，用拇指尖触碰鼻尖，摆动手指。”

00:02:54.990 --> 00:02:59.970
<v Ira Glass>OK, just pausing that again. Have you got what he's describing there? OK, good.
<v Ira Glass>好的，再暂停一下。明白他描述的动作了吗？好的，很好。

00:02:59.970 --> 00:03:18.890
<v Studs Terkel>"Now, this would provide a fitting message and ensure an explanation for the busybodies of America, who currently are wallowing in indignation, their favorite puddle. The busybodies are upset because Douglas, 67, married Cathleen Heffernan, 23. It was his fourth marriage, and she is his youngest bride."
<v Studs Terkel>“现在，这将提供一个恰当的信息，并给美国的那些好事者一个解释，他们目前正沉浸在义愤填膺的泥潭中，这是他们最喜欢的泥潭。好事者们很不满，因为67岁的道格拉斯娶了23岁的Cathleen Heffernan。这是他的第四次婚姻，而她是她最年轻的新娘。”

00:03:18.890 --> 00:03:28.850
<v Ira Glass>The issue here, in Royko's opinion, is whether this is really anybody's business at all-- which is, incidentally, the subject of today's radio show, our radio show, when to mind your own business.
<v Ira Glass>在Royko看来，这里的问题是这到底是否与任何人有关——顺便说一句，这正是今天广播节目的主题，我们的广播节目，什么时候该管好你自己的事。

00:03:28.850 --> 00:03:40.980
<v Studs Terkel>"The fact that a man they don't know married a girl they don't know, for reasons that are none of anyone else's business, has a terrible effect on the tempers of many people. Why this is, I don't know."
<v Studs Terkel>“一个他们不认识的人娶了一个他们不认识的女孩，理由与任何人无关，这事实对许多人的脾气产生了可怕的影响。为什么会这样，我不知道。”

00:03:40.980 --> 00:04:09.050
<v Ira Glass>Royko's column then quotes some things that he heard somebody say about the marriage on a Chicago call-in show. And then Royko turns to Congress, where a Democrat from Alabama called for a congressional investigation into Douglas's character because of this marriage, and said that he had heard that Douglas had been cruel to his previous wives. A congressman from Mississippi said that of all the courts troublesome decisions recently, this decision, to head down the quote, "highway of matrimony," has raised the most eyebrows.
<v Ira Glass>Royko的专栏接着引用了他从芝加哥的一个电话连线节目中听到的一些人对这次婚姻的评论。然后Royko转向国会，那里的一位来自阿拉巴马州的民主党人因为这次婚姻呼吁对道格拉斯的品格进行国会调查，并说他听说道格拉斯对前任妻子很残忍。一位来自密西西比州的国会议员说，在法院最近所有麻烦的判决中，这个踏上所谓“婚姻高速公路”的决定，最令人侧目。

00:04:09.050 --> 00:04:23.330
<v Studs Terkel>"And Representative Paul Finley, Republican of Illinois, said there should be a way to remove a justice from the bench without a trial for crimes or misdemeanors."
<v Studs Terkel>“伊利诺伊州的共和党众议员Paul Finley说，应该有一种无需审判即可罢免大法官职务的方法，即使没有犯罪或轻罪。”

00:04:23.330 --> 00:04:59.730
<v Ira Glass>OK, seriously, hearing this today, with the distance of a half century, this controversy feels so utterly about nothing-- so inconsequential. It makes you wonder, is this what the Anthony Weiner story is going to sound like, a half century from now? And yeah, OK, maybe it's a little icky for a 67-year-old to marry a 23-year-old, but these, you know, are consenting adults. These are people who are doing nothing against the laws of the United States of America, nothing that seems to merit congressional action. Anyway, Royko's column ends with several questions, he says, remain unanswered.
<v Ira Glass>好吧，说真的，今天听到这些，隔了半个世纪，这种争议感觉完全没什么大不了——太不重要了。这让人怀疑，五十年后，Anthony Weiner的故事听起来会是怎样的？好吧，也许让一个67岁的人娶一个23岁的人确实有点令人不适，但他们，你知道，是成年人，双方自愿。他们没有做任何违反美利坚合众国法律的事，也没有任何看起来值得国会采取行动的事。总之，Royko的专栏以几个悬而未决的问题结束。

00:04:59.730 --> 00:05:12.450
<v Studs Terkel>"How many marriages will a justice be allowed before he gets kicked off the bench? What will be an acceptable age difference in the marriage of a justice to a younger woman? Will a justice be permitted to marry an older woman?
<v Studs Terkel>“一位大法官在被踢出法官席之前，被允许结婚多少次？大法官与年轻女性结婚，可接受的年龄差是多少？大法官被允许娶比他年长的女性吗？”

00:05:12.450 --> 00:05:24.630
<v Studs Terkel>What does the Alabama congressman consider cruelty to a wife? Are tear gas, billy clubs, police dogs, and shotguns acceptable on the highway of matrimony, or only on the highway back home?"
<v Studs Terkel>“阿拉巴马州的国会议员认为什么是对妻子的残忍？催泪瓦斯、警棍、警犬和猎枪在婚姻高速公路上是可以接受的，还是只在回家的路上？”

00:05:24.630 --> 00:05:25.910
<v Mike Royko>[COUGHS]
<v Mike Royko>[咳嗽]

00:05:25.910 --> 00:05:29.550
<v Studs Terkel>Well, naturally, something happened here--
<v Studs Terkel>嗯，很自然地，这里发生了一些事——

00:05:29.550 --> 00:05:30.410
<v Mike Royko>[COUGHS] Yeah.
<v Mike Royko>[咳嗽] 是的。

00:05:30.410 --> 00:05:31.330
<v Studs Terkel>--when you wrote this.
<v Studs Terkel>——当你写这篇文章的时候。

00:05:31.330 --> 00:05:38.880
<v Mike Royko>Yeah. People got mad. The response was almost unanimously against my position.
<v Mike Royko>是的。人们很生气。反应几乎是一致反对我的立场。

00:05:38.880 --> 00:05:39.720
<v Studs Terkel>Now, what sort of madness?
<v Studs Terkel>那么，是哪种疯狂？

00:05:39.720 --> 00:05:57.930
<v Mike Royko>I wasn't surprised. Oh, well, there were some very funny letters. I found out that apparently more people are concerned about the Supreme Court justice's marriage than they are about what he does the rest of the time that he's on the bench.
<v Mike Royko>我不感到惊讶。哦，好吧，有一些非常有趣的信。我发现显然有更多的人关心最高法院大法官的婚姻，而不是关心他在法官席上其余时间所做的事情。

00:05:57.930 --> 00:06:03.030
<v Mike Royko>One woman wrote me a letter and said that she surely wouldn't vote for him the next time he ran for election.
<v Mike Royko>一位女士给我写了一封信，说她下次选举时肯定不会投他的票。

00:06:03.030 --> 00:06:03.980
<v Studs Terkel>[LAUGHS]
<v Studs Terkel>[笑声]

00:06:03.980 --> 00:06:06.990
<v Mike Royko>[LAUGHS] I don't think she'd heard of him before. And--
<v Mike Royko>[笑声] 我想她在那之前可能没听说过他。而且——

00:06:06.990 --> 00:06:18.000
<v Ira Glass>This next part of the recording I really just love. They haul in staffers at the radio station-- basically just grab them from down the hall-- to read some of the letters that Royko got after he wrote this column.
<v Ira Glass>录音的接下来这一部分我真的很喜欢。他们把电台的工作人员叫来——基本上就是从走廊里把他们抓过来——读一些Royko写完这篇专栏后收到的信件。

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:26.380
<v Studs Terkel>So now we got-- here are letters in response to Mike Royko's column, if we may start. And then Mike, perhaps, you could read your comment. You have a little comment at the end of each one.
<v Studs Terkel>所以现在我们有——这是回应Mike Royko专栏的信件，如果我们能开始的话。然后Mike，也许你可以读你的评论。你在每一封信的最后都有一个小评论。

00:06:26.380 --> 00:06:26.870
<v Mike Royko>No, you read it.
<v Mike Royko>不，你来读。

00:06:26.870 --> 00:06:28.090
<v Studs Terkel>No, you do-- no, the comment.
<v Studs Terkel>不，你来——不，评论部分。

00:06:28.090 --> 00:06:29.450
<v Mike Royko>I-- I can't read it.
<v Mike Royko>我——我读不了。

00:06:29.450 --> 00:06:35.800
<v Studs Terkel>All right. So I will be Mike Royko. This is Jimmy Jake, an older man. However, you read. This is Jake, here.
<v Studs Terkel>好吧。那我来做Mike Royko。这是Jimmy Jake，一位老人。不过，你来读。这是Jake。

00:06:35.800 --> 00:07:01.300
<v Male Staffer>"It is hard to believe that you were serious in your column about Justice Douglas. I'm 62. And ever since I read about this bit, I've been trying to figure out how I could tactfully and with justification approach some or even one of the cute young things that populate the building I'm in. No matter how I phrase my approach, I seem to hear the same words. 'Why, you dirty old man.' Now then, this makes me wonder, how does a 67-year-old man make the approach?"
<v Male Staffer>“很难相信你在关于道格拉斯大法官的专栏中是认真的。我62岁了。自从我读到这个消息，我就一直在想怎么能得体地、有理由地接近我所在大楼里那些可爱的年轻姑娘。无论我怎么表达，我似乎总是听到同样的话：‘你这个肮脏的老头。’那么，这让我怀疑，一个67岁的人是怎么接近对方的？”

00:07:01.300 --> 00:07:09.840
<v Studs Terkel>"Oh, well, I assume he said something like, will you marry me? What have you been thinking of saying?"
<v Studs Terkel>“哦，好吧，我假设他说了类似‘你愿意嫁给我吗？’之类的话。你一直在想说什么？”

00:07:09.840 --> 00:07:12.900
<v Studs Terkel>And then a girl named Jane was indignant. Kathy?
<v Studs Terkel>然后一个叫Jane的女孩很气愤。Kathy？

00:07:12.900 --> 00:07:31.410
<v Kathy>"You have an obligation to the reading public as well as to yourself to stick with the truth. And truth comes from God. And what has Justice Douglas done with God's commandments? People disgustingly, and rightly so, talk about Liz Taylor. Douglas is just one step behind her, making a mockery of marriage.
<v Kathy>“你对阅读大众和对自己都有义务坚持真理。真理来自上帝。道格拉斯大法官把上帝的诫命置于何地？人们令人厌恶地，而且正确地谈论Liz Taylor。道格拉斯只比她落后一步，在嘲弄婚姻。”

00:07:31.410 --> 00:07:47.280
<v Kathy>Do you advocate what Liz Taylor has done? This country's laws are based on Christian doctrine and morality. And if a Supreme Court judge openly and continually disregards those laws, what is the common man to think about regarding the sanctity of marriage?"
<v Kathy>“你主张Liz Taylor所做的事吗？这个国家的法律基于基督教教义和道德。如果最高法院法官公开且持续地无视这些法律，普通人会对婚姻的神圣性怎么想？”

00:07:47.280 --> 00:07:51.100
<v Kathy>[LAUGHTER]
<v Kathy>[笑声]

00:07:51.100 --> 00:07:51.420
<v Studs Terkel>Well--
<v Studs Terkel>嗯——

00:07:51.420 --> 00:08:01.980
<v Ira Glass>All right, I'm going to stop the tape there. I assume that they're laughing mostly because of the sheer moxie that their colleague is bringing to her performance of that letter. Studs is usually very tolerant of people's religious beliefs.
<v Ira Glass>好了，我要在那儿暂停录音。我假设他们笑主要是因为他们的同事在读那封信时表现出的那种纯粹的勇气。Studs通常对人们的宗教信仰非常宽容。

00:08:01.980 --> 00:08:19.220
<v Ira Glass>Her letter does get to the heart of controversy, though. Douglas was a very liberal voice on the liberal Warren Court. And lots of conservatives saw this marriage as just another indication of permissive liberal values and what was wrong with the whole direction of the country in 1966. Anyway, here's one more letter.
<v Ira Glass>不过，她的信确实触及了争议的核心。道格拉斯是自由派Warren法院中一个非常自由的声音。许多保守派认为这次婚姻只是自由派价值观放纵的又一迹象，也是1966年整个国家方向错误的表现。总之，还有一封信。

00:08:19.220 --> 00:08:23.870
<v Studs Terkel>And finally, Marty Robinson was busy announcing. Now, Marty, you're a doctor in Oak Park.
<v Studs Terkel>最后，Marty Robinson在忙着发表意见。Marty，你是橡树园的医生。

00:08:23.870 --> 00:08:32.230
<v Male Staffer>"Now, your thinking in your Douglas column is the kind of fuzzy mindedness that is seized upon by many to do anything they wish in the name of freedom."
<v Male Staffer>“你对道格拉斯专栏的想法，是那种被许多人打着自由的旗号利用来做任何他们想做的事的糊涂思想。”

00:08:32.230 --> 00:08:33.310
<v Male Staffer>[CHUCKLING]
<v Male Staffer>[轻笑]

00:08:33.310 --> 00:08:43.710
<v Studs Terkel>Spoken like an authoritative doctor. And Mike replies, "Getting married four times isn't exactly my idea of having freedom." That's a good one. Thank you, ladies and gentlemen.
<v Studs Terkel>说得像个权威的医生。Mike回复道：“结四次婚并不完全是我心目中的自由。”说得好。谢谢大家。

00:08:43.710 --> 00:08:48.030
<v Studs Terkel>Now, Mike, did you receive any letters praising you for the column?
<v Studs Terkel>Mike，你有没有收到任何赞扬你这篇专栏的信？

00:08:48.030 --> 00:08:50.140
<v Mike Royko>A couple of dirty old men liked it.
<v Mike Royko>几个肮脏的老头喜欢它。

00:08:50.140 --> 00:08:57.470
<v Mike Royko>[LAUGHS]
<v Mike Royko>[笑声]

00:08:57.470 --> 00:09:11.100
<v Ira Glass>Can I just say-- OK, it's annoying, the self-righteousness that people have in condemning this marriage, right? But it's also kind of harsh, the self-righteousness that Royko has in criticizing them. After all, like, sure, what happens in this marriage is nobody's business but their own.
<v Ira Glass>我可以插一句吗——好吧，人们在谴责这段婚姻时表现出的自以为是令人讨厌，对吧？但Royko在批评他们时表现出的自以为是也有点刻薄。毕竟，确实，这段婚姻里发生了什么除了他们自己谁也不管。

00:09:11.100 --> 00:09:24.920
<v Ira Glass>But who among us, I would ask you, who among us hears about a 67-year-old man marrying somebody a third his age without feeling like a little twinge of judginess, right? Like, who doesn't have feelings about that? Who is above that?
<v Ira Glass>但我想问你们，我们中间有谁听到一个67岁的人娶了一个年龄只有他三分之一的人，而不感到一点点评判的刺痛感呢？对吧？谁没有这种感觉？谁能凌驾于此？

00:09:24.920 --> 00:09:41.360
<v Ira Glass>Which is to say, it's hard minding your own business. We're human beings. We're programmed to make judgments about every situation that we walk into and hear about. It is hard not to be nosy. It is hard not to want to know more. That is being alive.
<v Ira Glass>也就是说，管好自己的事很难。我们是人类。我们被编程为对我们进入和听到的每一个情况做出判断。很难不爱管闲事。很难不想知道更多。这就是生活。

00:09:41.360 --> 00:09:59.600
<v Ira Glass>And today on our program, we have stories of people not minding their own business. Well, actually, in these stories, it's big institutions not minding their business and busybodying their way into all kinds of stuff, into what kind of underwear is being worn by cheerleaders, into what is happening in jail hallways in cell blocks.
<v Ira Glass>今天在我们的节目中，我们有关于人们没有管好自己事的故事。嗯，实际上，在这些故事中，是大型机构没有管好自己的事，并以“好事者”的方式介入了各种事情，介入啦啦队员穿什么样的内衣，介入监狱走廊牢房里发生的事情。

00:09:59.600 --> 00:10:12.460
<v Ira Glass>From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>来自WBEZ芝加哥，这是《美国生活》。我是Ira Glass。请继续收听。

00:10:12.460 --> 00:10:35.310
<v Ira Glass>Act One, Jill House Rules. In January of this year, a woman who went by the name of Lacy T filed a lawsuit against the Oakland raiders for failing to pay her minimum wage. Lacy was a cheerleader for the team, a rookie. NFL cheerleaders generally make about $1,500 for the entire season, although some make as little as $850 for the entire season.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，Jill的家规。今年一月，一位名叫Lacy T的女士对奥克兰突袭者队提起诉讼，指控他们没有支付她最低工资。Lacy是该队的啦啦队员，一名新人。NFL啦啦队员整个赛季通常收入约1500美元，尽管有些人整个赛季仅赚850美元。

00:10:35.310 --> 00:11:02.600
<v Ira Glass>Just a few weeks after Lacy, another cheerleader filed a suit, this one against the Cincinnati Bengals. Then, cheerleaders with the Buffalo Bills, New York Jets, Tampa Bay Buccaneers, women from five NFL cheerleaders teams were suing for higher pay. What's interesting is that the way the cheerleaders are trying to win is to argue that the teams exert so much control over their lives, they are so up in their business, that the cheerleaders deserve to be considered employees. If they are considered employees, then they have to be paid minimum wage.
<v Ira Glass>在Lacy之后仅几周，另一位啦啦队员提起了诉讼，这次是针对辛辛那提孟加拉虎队。然后，布法罗比尔队、纽约喷气机队、坦帕湾海盗队的啦啦队员，来自五个NFL啦啦队团队的女性都在起诉要求更高的工资。有趣的是，啦啦队员们试图获胜的方式是辩称球队对她们的生活行使了如此多的控制权，以至于她们如此深入地干涉她们的事务，啦啦队员们值得被视为雇员。如果她们被视为雇员，那么她们就必须获得最低工资。

00:11:02.600 --> 00:11:12.100
<v Ira Glass>So the lawsuits also include a bunch of internal documents that describe how much up in their business the franchises are. Here's Chana Joffe-Walt.
<v Ira Glass>所以诉讼还包括一堆内部文件，描述了球队对她们的生活干涉有多深。这是Chana Joffe-Walt的报道。

00:11:12.100 --> 00:11:26.960
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>It was Page 18 that really got me, the Buffalo Jills lawsuit. The team is the Buffalo Bills, so the cheerleaders are the Jills. And on Page 18 of these legal documents, they're describing a Jills rulebook the team gives out to each squad member.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>真正让我震惊的是第18页，关于布法罗Jills的诉讼。球队是布法罗比尔队，所以啦啦队员被称为Jills。在这些法律文件的第18页，他们描述了球队发给每位队员的Jills规则手册。

00:11:26.960 --> 00:12:01.990
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>It says, quote, "The extensive rulebook set forth by defendants includes rules on how much bread to eat at a formal dinner, how to properly eat soup, how much to tip restaurant waiters, wedding etiquette, how to properly wash intimate areas, and how often to change tampons." And then, right here as evidence, they include the actual handbook-- the "glamour requirement section," two pages on conversation starters, 17 rules for what they call "general hygiene and lady body maintenance."
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>它写道，引用：“被告制定的详尽规则手册包括关于在正式晚宴上吃多少面包、如何正确喝汤、给餐厅服务员多少小费、婚礼礼仪、如何正确清洗私密部位以及多久更换一次卫生棉条的规定。”然后，就在这里作为证据，他们包括了实际手册——“魅力要求部分”，两页关于谈话开头的话题，17条关于他们所谓的“个人卫生和女性身体保养”的规则。

00:12:01.990 --> 00:12:18.510
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>I think what's so bizarre about reading these rules as an outsider, someone who works in an office every day, is it's really hard to imagine your boss talking to you about this stuff. Let me show you what I mean with my boss. I asked my boss, Ira, to read some of these rules. Hey, Ira.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>我想作为一个外人，一个每天在办公室工作的人，读到这些规则真是太不可思议了，真的很难想象你的老板跟你谈论这些事。让我通过我的老板演示一下我的意思。我请我的老板Ira读了一些这些规则。嘿，Ira。

00:12:18.510 --> 00:12:19.540
<v Ira Glass>Hey there, Chana.
<v Ira Glass>嘿，Chana。

00:12:19.540 --> 00:12:24.270
<v Chana Joffe- Walt>OK. So imagine your boss walking into your cubicle and saying--
<v Chana Joffe- Walt>好的。想象一下你的老板走进你的隔间说——

00:12:24.270 --> 00:12:36.590
<v Ira Glass>All right, here we go. "Stay away from frosted lipsticks and eye shadows. Management will determine your proper color analysis. No slouching breasts-- support as needed."
<v Ira Glass>好吧，我们开始。“远离磨砂质地的口红和眼影。管理层将确定你正确的色彩分析。不得含胸驼背——必要时给予支撑。”

00:12:36.590 --> 00:12:37.320
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Yeah, that's weird.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>是啊，那很怪。

00:12:37.320 --> 00:12:38.120
<v Ira Glass>That's weird.
<v Ira Glass>那很怪。

00:12:38.120 --> 00:12:43.270
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Keep going, though. Keep going. So here are some more actual rules from actual cheerleader handbooks.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>继续读下去。所以这里还有一些来自实际啦啦队员手册的实际规则。

00:12:43.270 --> 00:13:04.520
<v Ira Glass>"Never apply makeup or fuss with hair front of people. If it's absolutely necessary you reapply, freshen up, go to the ladies' room. And do not hang out and talk while there. Beware, other women will judge you in there, too. Always say excuse me when you burp, sneeze, or cough, even if you think there isn't anyone around.
<v Ira Glass>“永远不要在人前化妆或整理头发。如果绝对需要重新化妆，去洗手间整理。不要在里面闲逛聊天。小心，其他女性也会在里面评判你。打嗝、打喷嚏或咳嗽时一定要说对不起，即使你认为周围没有人。”

00:13:04.520 --> 00:13:17.240
<v Ira Glass>Napkins-- when you leave the table at the end of the meal, place your napkin loosely next to your plate. It should not be crumpled or twisted, which would reveal untidiness or nervousness, respectively."
<v Ira Glass>“餐巾——用餐结束离开餐桌时，将餐巾松散地放在盘子旁边。它不应该被揉皱或扭曲，这会分别显示出凌乱或紧张。”

00:13:17.240 --> 00:13:28.450
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>There's a lot of rules about the cheerleaders' bodies, which I get. It's a job that depends on your body. But these rules get deep into your mind, like these ones. Read these, Ira.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>有很多关于啦啦队员身体的规则，我明白。这是一份依赖你身体的工作。但这些规则深入到你的思想，比如这些。读一下这些，Ira。

00:13:28.450 --> 00:13:43.390
<v Ira Glass>OK. "Please, think before you speak and always pay attention and listen. Ask yourself, 'Is it likely that this person will be interested in what I'm about to bring up?' Do not be overly opinionated about anything.
<v Ira Glass>好的。“请在说话前思考，并时刻注意倾听。问问自己，‘这个人会对我要提起的话题感兴趣吗？’不要对任何事情过于固执己见。”

00:13:43.390 --> 00:14:01.700
<v Ira Glass>Do not complain about anything. Ever hang out with a whiner? It's exhausting and boring. Watch other poor manners or nervous habits, such as nail biting, knuckle-slash-neck cracking, excessive sniffling, and too many arm movements."
<v Ira Glass>“不要抱怨任何事情。曾经和爱抱怨的人待在一起吗？这既累人又无聊。注意其他糟糕的礼仪或紧张的习惯，比如咬指甲、掰关节/脖子、频繁吸鼻子和过多的手臂动作。”

00:14:01.700 --> 00:14:06.340
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>I like imagining what the author was picturing with that last one. "Too many arm movements."
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>我喜欢想象作者写最后一条时脑海里在想什么。“过多的手臂动作。”

00:14:06.340 --> 00:14:07.110
<v Ira Glass>Yeah.
<v Ira Glass>是啊。

00:14:07.110 --> 00:14:27.870
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>OK. So I didn't really know what to make of these. It's hard to imagine the NFL players have handbooks that outline how to eat bread politely and wash their genitals. But I don't know. Maybe no cheerleaders actually talk about these rules, or no one enforces them. Maybe cheerleading manuals are essentially employee handbooks that nobody ever reads.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>好吧。所以我真的不知道该怎么看待这些。很难想象NFL球员有规定如何礼貌地吃面包和清洗生殖器的手册。但我不知道。也许没有啦啦队员真的讨论这些规则，或者没有人执行它们。也许啦啦队手册本质上是没人读过的员工手册。

00:14:27.870 --> 00:14:40.680
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>So I ran a bunch of them by a couple current and former cheerleaders, who told me, yeah, we had lots of rules like that. And we knew those rules. But most of them also added, the rules were not a big deal.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>所以我向几位现任和前任啦啦队员询问了一些，她们告诉我，是的，我们确实有很多那样的规则。我们知道那些规则。但她们大多数人也补充说，这些规则没什么大不了的。

00:14:40.680 --> 00:14:43.910
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Carly Walko cheered for the Redskins and the Eagles.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Carly Walko曾为红皮队和老鹰队欢呼。

00:14:43.910 --> 00:14:59.550
<v Carly Walko>I mean, of course some of them were frustrating, like your nails a certain length or whatever. And, I mean, you just have to believe that every rule's there because there's been a circumstance in the past that has had to put it there. So you would think, nail length, who cares? Everyone keeps their nails relatively reasonably long.
<v Carly Walko>我是说，当然其中一些令人沮丧，比如指甲长度之类的。而且，我的意思是，你只需要相信每一条规则的存在都是因为过去发生过某种不得不制定它的情况。所以你会想，指甲长度，谁在乎呢？每个人都把指甲保持在相对合理的长度。

00:14:59.550 --> 00:15:17.000
<v Carly Walko>There must have been some girl along the way who had like 8-inch nails and probably stabbed some girl in the back doing a lift or something. And so now they have to say you can't have your nails be past a certain point. Which seems silly, but someone 10 years ago got hurt on the field because a girl's nails were too long. Do you know what I'm saying?
<v Carly Walko>肯定有哪个女孩一路走来留了8英寸长的指甲，可能在做托举动作时戳伤了某个女孩。所以现在他们必须说指甲不能超过某个长度。这看起来很傻，但10年前确实有人因为女孩指甲太长而在场上受伤了。你知道我的意思吗？

00:15:17.000 --> 00:15:27.080
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Other cheerleaders I talked to said the same thing. The rules may seem weird from the outside, but really, they're not. And they didn't have a problem with them.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>其他我交谈过的啦啦队员也说了同样的话。这些规则从外面看可能很奇怪，但实际上并非如此。她们对这些规则没有意见。

00:15:27.080 --> 00:15:43.170
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>But I wasn't confident that was a completely honest take. Cheerleaders, after all, are hired to promote their teams. That's their job. So even if they did find the rules stupid or way up in their business, why would they talk to some stranger about that on the radio?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>但我并不确定这是一个完全诚实的看法。毕竟，啦啦队员是被雇佣来推广她们球队的。这是她们的工作。所以即使她们觉得这些规则很蠢或干涉太多，为什么要在广播里对陌生人说这些呢？

00:15:43.170 --> 00:15:57.630
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>But I realized I do have someone to ask about these documents who knows this world and could speak with me honestly about it-- my colleague, This American Life producer Robyn Semien. Robyn was an NBA cheerleader.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>但我意识到我确实有一个可以询问这些文件的人，她了解这个世界，并且可以诚实地与我谈论它——我的同事，This American Life制片人Robyn Semien。Robyn曾是一名NBA啦啦队员。

00:15:57.630 --> 00:16:02.370
<v Robyn Semien>I had a great time on the Lakers. I really did. It was fun. It was fun.
<v Robyn Semien>我在湖人队度过了一段美好的时光。我真的觉得很有趣。很有趣。

00:16:02.370 --> 00:16:20.590
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Robyn was a Laker Girl in college. After 20 years of dance classes and cheer camps, she made it to the Lakers, which is a big deal. I feel like I need to say that, because Robyn will not. She made me ask three times before she told me she beat out hundreds of other women for her spot.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Robyn上大学时是湖人女孩。经过20年的舞蹈课和啦啦队训练营，她成功进入了湖人队，这可是件大事。我觉得我有必要提一下，因为Robyn自己不会说。她让我问了三次，才告诉我她击败了数百名其他女性才获得了这个位置。

00:16:20.590 --> 00:16:39.520
<v Robyn Semien>In the end, it's 16 of us were chosen-- three brunettes and three blonds and two redheads, maybe. And so I was one of two black girls that were hired. So, like, I knew that this was just kind of-- that there were roles, that I was auditioning for a part. And that was fine.
<v Robyn Semien>最终，我们有16个人入选——可能是三个棕色头发的，三个金发的，两个红发的。我是被雇佣的两个黑人女孩之一。所以，我知道这只是某种——我有角色，我在为一个角色试镜。这没关系。

00:16:39.520 --> 00:16:50.260
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>When I read the guidelines and handbooks to Robyn, she just kind of shrugged. We were there representing an enormous franchise, she told me. Of course there were rules about everything.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>当我向Robyn读那些准则和手册时，她只是耸了耸肩。她告诉我，我们在那里代表一个庞大的特许经营权。当然关于一切都有规则。

00:16:50.260 --> 00:17:14.000
<v Robyn Semien>Yeah, there were tons of rules. A main one was you can't date the players, which meant-- like, you can't date the players, which really meant you can't sleep with the players. But also you can't date them. But also you can't be seen anywhere alone with them. Like, you cannot-- there's just no crossover between what we are doing as the Laker Girls and what the Laker team was doing.
<v Robyn Semien>是的，规则成吨。主要的一条是你不能和球员约会，这意味着——比如，你不能和球员约会，实际上意味着你不能和球员上床。但你也不能和他们约会。而且你也不能在任何地方单独和他们一起被看到。就像，你不能——作为湖人女孩我们所做的和湖人队所做的事情之间没有任何交叉。

00:17:14.000 --> 00:17:16.250
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>So you didn't have any relationships with any of the players.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>所以你和任何球员都没有过任何关系。

00:17:16.250 --> 00:17:19.980
<v Robyn Semien>None. I mean, none.
<v Robyn Semien>没有。我的意思是，完全没有。

00:17:19.980 --> 00:17:31.300
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Robyn says everyone knew that rule. And they didn't need to read it in a handbook, because they had Lisa. Lisa Estrada was and still is the director of the Laker Girls.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Robyn说每个人都知道那条规则。她们不需要在手册里读到它，因为她们有Lisa。Lisa Estrada是，现在仍然是湖人女孩的总监。

00:17:31.300 --> 00:17:49.010
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>It was Lisa who told the squad which kind of lipstick to wear, how much, which mascara, how long their nails should be. It was Lisa who did weigh-ins before games. It was Lisa who told Robyn to straighten her hair so it'd look like Suzanne Somers from Three's Company . Lisa was a boss you remember.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>正是Lisa告诉队员们涂哪种口红，涂多少，用哪种睫毛膏，指甲该留多长。是Lisa在比赛前进行体重检查。是Lisa告诉Robyn要把头发拉直，这样看起来就像《三人行》里的Suzanne Somers。Lisa是一个你会记住的老板。

00:17:49.010 --> 00:18:22.730
<v Robyn Semien>She was-- I mean, she's a spitfire. And she was really funny, and also kind of really kind of scary. In my mind she was, like, spending all day every day like in some meeting with, like, Jerry Buss like the owner of the Lakers. Just she was always seeming to come from some important meeting or something important about our dances or our image or what the Lakers organization needed from us meant.
<v Robyn Semien>她——我的意思是，她是个火药桶。她真的很风趣，但也确实有点吓人。在我看来，她好像整天整天都在和Jerry Buss（湖人老板）开会。她总是看起来刚从某个重要的会议，或者是关于我们的舞蹈、我们的形象，或者是湖人组织需要我们做什么的会议中走出来。

00:18:22.730 --> 00:18:33.350
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>When they went out on appearances-- say a Staples office supply store would want four Laker girls for their grand opening-- they would offer good money. And Lisa would say, you can go but--
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>当她们出去参加活动时——比如史泰博办公用品商店想要四个湖人女孩参加开业典礼——他们会提供不错的报酬。而Lisa会说，你们可以去，但是——

00:18:33.350 --> 00:18:40.830
<v Robyn Semien>I expect that you all conduct yourselves appropriately. And don't ever forget what you're representing.
<v Robyn Semien>我期望你们所有人都举止得体。永远不要忘记你们代表着什么。

00:18:40.830 --> 00:18:42.370
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>What were you representing?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>你们代表着什么？

00:18:42.370 --> 00:18:46.420
<v Robyn Semien>I don't-- um, class.
<v Robyn Semien>我不——嗯，阶级感。

00:18:46.420 --> 00:18:48.580
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>You were classy.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>你们很优雅。

00:18:48.580 --> 00:18:52.720
<v Robyn Semien>Yes. Yeah, yeah.
<v Robyn Semien>是的。是啊，是啊。

00:18:52.720 --> 00:18:55.820
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Did you understand what that meant?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>你明白那意味着什么吗？

00:18:55.820 --> 00:19:02.540
<v Robyn Semien>Kind of. It just meant that we weren't slutty.
<v Robyn Semien>算是吧。这只是意味着我们不是那种轻浮的人。

00:19:02.540 --> 00:19:18.570
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>There was a line. Lisa wanted the line to be blindingly bright. And that's what the rules were about. One side of the line was classy. The other side was slutty. Lisa wanted you to be on the right side of the line. And that made sense to Robyn.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>有一条界限。Lisa想让这条界限亮得刺眼。这就是规则的意义所在。界限的一边是优雅。另一边是轻浮。Lisa想让你待在界限的右边。这对Robyn来说很有道理。

00:19:18.570 --> 00:19:48.630
<v Robyn Semien>I feel like in general, as a dancer, if you're, like, trying to make it as a professional dancer in Los Angeles, you're coming across a lot of questionable opportunities. You're like, do I or don't I want to put on stilettos and a bikini and be in that music video, because I could totally do it, but-- so it felt a little bit like a Disney version of-- yeah, classy but, like, sexy classy. Sure.
<v Robyn Semien>我觉得总的来说，作为一个舞者，如果你试图在洛杉矶成为一名职业舞者，你会遇到很多可疑的机会。你会想，我到底要不要穿上细高跟鞋和比基尼出现在那个音乐录影带里，因为我完全做得到，但是——所以这感觉有点像迪士尼版本的——是的，优雅，但就像，性感优雅。当然。

00:19:48.630 --> 00:19:49.100
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>[LAUGHS]
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>[笑声]

00:19:49.100 --> 00:19:53.690
<v Robyn Semien>Yes. Sequins and hot pink all over your face. Whatever you want.
<v Robyn Semien>是的。满脸亮片和亮粉。随你怎么想。

00:19:53.690 --> 00:19:56.010
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Right. But don't sleep with any of the players.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>对。但不要和任何球员上床。

00:19:56.010 --> 00:20:05.060
<v Robyn Semien>But do not fornicate with the players, yeah.
<v Robyn Semien>但不要和球员通奸，是的。

00:20:05.060 --> 00:20:23.920
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>It made sense to Robyn that they shouldn't sleep with the players. It made sense that Lisa told her what kind of push-up bra to wear and to get it specifically from Frederick's of Hollywood. It made sense that Lisa wanted her to be careful while dancing that she was doing her girl-next-door face and not a diva sex face.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Robyn觉得她们不应该和球员上床很有道理。Lisa告诉她穿什么样的聚拢文胸，并且必须具体在好莱坞的Frederick's买，这很有道理。Lisa想让她在跳舞时小心，保持邻家女孩的表情而不是那种诱惑的表情，这很有道理。

00:20:23.920 --> 00:20:29.300
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>That all seemed reasonable. But Lisa had a lot of rules. They didn't all make sense.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>这一切看起来都很合理。但Lisa有很多规则。并不是每一条都有道理。

00:20:29.300 --> 00:20:35.210
<v Robyn Semien>One of the rules that we had was that we weren't allowed to drink water out of water bottles.
<v Robyn Semien>我们有的规则之一是我们不被允许从水瓶里喝水。

00:20:35.210 --> 00:20:36.060
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Why?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>为什么？

00:20:36.060 --> 00:21:02.010
<v Robyn Semien>This was like a rule. Every single rule came from Lisa. Totally, like, normal-but-a-little-intimidating Lisa would come to us and say, all right, no. OK, but no drinking water at these appearances. And then she explained that it was like because it was too provocative to drink out of a water bottle. Like, it just could connote something that would be--
<v Robyn Semien>这就像一条规则。每一条规则都来自Lisa。完全像，正常但有点令人生畏的Lisa会来到我们面前说，好吧，不。好的，但在这些活动中不准喝水。然后她解释说这是因为从水瓶里喝水太有挑逗性了。就像，它只会暗示一些会让——

00:21:02.010 --> 00:21:02.960
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>What? What?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>什么？什么？

00:21:02.960 --> 00:21:05.230
<v Robyn Semien>I know.
<v Robyn Semien>我知道。

00:21:05.230 --> 00:21:05.900
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>What's provocative about it?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>它有什么挑逗性的？

00:21:05.900 --> 00:21:32.010
<v Robyn Semien>Yeah, I don't know. I don't know. I don't think anyone was like, what do you mean? We were all just like, yeah, OK. Immediately, like, I'm trying to imagine what she's imagining. And it just suddenly seems like a everything gets slow motion and we're like, drinking water in this way that is just inappropriate. It's like suddenly, like, rated R water drinking.
<v Robyn Semien>是啊，我不知道。我不知道。我觉得当时没人会想，你是什么意思？我们都只是说，是的，好的。我立刻试图想象她在想象什么。突然间感觉一切都变慢动作了，我们就像在用一种不恰当的方式喝水。这就像突然间变成了限制级的喝水。

00:21:32.010 --> 00:21:35.010
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>The word Robyn remembers Lisa using was "porny."
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Robyn记得Lisa用的词是“色情”。

00:21:35.010 --> 00:21:57.800
<v Robyn Semien>Yeah, it's porny. Yeah. Or at least to Lisa it is, or someone in the greater organization at one of her many afternoon meetings has told her, like, whoa, slow down on all the water drinking. Let's keep it wholesome. I don't know. I don't know.
<v Robyn Semien>是的，就是色情。是的。或者至少对Lisa来说是这样，或者在她的某次下午会议中，组织里的某个人告诉她，哇，少喝点水。我们保持健康一点。我不知道。我不知道。

00:21:57.800 --> 00:22:10.520
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>If there is a line between classy and porny, cheerleading more than any other job I can think of is pushing right up against that line. It is dancing with pom-poms all over the line.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>如果优雅和色情之间有一条界限，啦啦队是我能想到的比任何其他工作都更贴近那条界限的。这就是在那条线上挥舞着绒球跳舞。

00:22:10.520 --> 00:22:25.400
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>So Robyn says the rules were there to keep things from accidentally crossing over. They're there to provide context. This thing we are doing is professional and classy. So even when Lisa's rules seemed a little crazy, Robyn went with it.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>所以Robyn说规则的存在是为了防止意外越界。它们在那里是为了提供背景。我们所做的这件事是专业且优雅的。所以即使当Lisa的规则看起来有点疯狂时，Robyn也照做了。

00:22:25.400 --> 00:22:32.490
<v Robyn Semien>She was trying to keep us safe-- appropriate and protected.
<v Robyn Semien>她在试图保护我们——得体且受保护。

00:22:32.490 --> 00:22:36.220
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Dd you feel that? Did you appreciate, like, she's looking out for us.
